+## 21. Withdrawing a result
+
+A result that should never have counted is withdrawn with
+`voidMatch({ matchId, reason, at })` while the tournament is still active.
+The match moves to `voided` and keeps the results it was completed with, so
+the record of what was claimed survives the correction. The call answers with
+a withdrawal record: `match`, `tournamentId`, the trimmed `reason`, `at`, the
+`rebuilt` score records in player id order, and an explanation whose details
+carry every rebuilt player's total before and after.
+
+Withdrawal is not a subtraction. A score record carries a running total, a
+win/loss/draw count, a live streak, the best streak reached and the reason
+behind every award, and all of those depend on the order results arrived in.
+Take one match away and a streak bonus somewhere later may never have been
+earned. So each player the voided match touched has this tournament's score
+rebuilt from the matches that still stand, oldest settlement first, every
+result scored again through the tournament's scoring config against the score
+built so far.
+
+A player left with nothing keeps a record, zeroed and stamped at the moment of
+the withdrawal. Players the match never touched are not rebuilt at all: their
+own results have not moved.
+
+`MatchVoided` carries the reason on the match stream, and a projector rebuilds
+the same way, so a journal replayed later reaches the totals the live run
+reached. Rewards are a different matter: a completed tournament refuses a
+withdrawal, because its board has been ranked and its pool may already be
+paid.
+
+A tournament report counts what was pulled: hand `buildTournamentReport` the
+tournament's matches and it reports `withdrawn`.
+
+Operators reach the same correction over HTTP with `POST /matches/:id/void`,
+from the CLI with `match:void`, and from the SDK with `matches.voidMatch`.
